We now have enough evidence to scope the SCENE transform-toolbar/gizmo pass
correctly.

IMPORTANT CORRECTION:

SCENE does NOT have a Blender-style transform gizmo.

But it DOES already have:

    viewport raycast picking
    direct object dragging
    X/Z drag on normal left-drag
    Y drag via Shift+drag
    orbit / fly / pan
    face-paint picking
    texture-stamp picking

So this is NOT "build picking from scratch".

And it is NOT "migrate Scene onto usePreviewViewport first".

We should extend the EXISTING App scene renderer with the RIG-style gizmo.

==================================================
1. DO NOT MIGRATE SCENE TO usePreviewViewport
==================================================

Keep Scene on its existing App-owned THREE renderer.

The Scene renderer already owns many Scene-specific behaviours:

    meshMap
    decorMap
    Raycaster
    face paint
    texture stamp face picking
    locked/hidden pick filtering
    X/Z direct dragging
    Shift+drag Y
    orbit camera
    fly camera
    PS2 camera preview
    sky/decor helpers
    point lights
    camera gizmos
    snapping interaction

Migrating all of that into usePreviewViewport is a much larger project than
adding transform handles.

It would also put the application's PRIMARY editor through a renderer migration
solely to inherit a gizmo.

Do not do that.

==================================================
2. CORRECT THE MISLEADING COMMENT
==================================================

Current updateObj comment says:

    every path that moves an object goes through updateObj —
    the viewport gizmo, the OBJECT tab fields and the context menu alike

That is currently false.

There is no Scene gizmo.

And the current viewport drag directly does:

    grp.position...
    objsRef.current mutation
    setScene(...)

rather than calling updateObj.

Correct the comment as part of this pass.

More importantly, do not build the new gizmo using another direct state-writing
path.

The new gizmo MUST call updateObj.

==================================================
3. CURRENT SCENE PICKING IS ALREADY GOOD
==================================================

Reuse the existing Scene raycaster.

It already does:

    const r = mount.getBoundingClientRect()

    mouse2d.x = ...
    mouse2d.y = ...

and raycasts only actual model meshes collected from meshMap.

It also correctly excludes:

    hidden layers
    locked layers
    noSelect meshes

Do not create a second independent picking system.

Extend the existing onDown path.

==================================================
4. ADD SCENE TRANSFORM STATE
==================================================

Introduce editor-only Scene transform state, analogous to RIG:

    sceneXform = 'move' | 'rotate' | 'scale'
    sceneAxis  = null | 'x' | 'y' | 'z'

These are UI/editor state only.

Do not persist them into scene objects or BAS.

Default:

    move
    axis null

==================================================
5. PORT THE RIG TOOLBAR VISUALLY
==================================================

Add the same compact transform toolbar in the Scene viewport:

    MOVE
    ROTATE
    SCALE

    X
    Y
    Z

Use the same icons, dimensions and general styling as RIG so the application
has one transform language.

Do not keep two unrelated transform tool designs.

If RIG has +/- nudge controls, Scene may have them too, but wire them through
updateObj and current selection.

==================================================
6. ADD ONE GIZMO TO THE EXISTING SCENE THREE SCENE
==================================================

Inside the App scene renderer setup, create:

    const transformGizmo = new THREE.Group()

Add it to the existing THREE scene.

Reuse/adapt the RIG primitive approach:

MOVE:
    three axis arrows

ROTATE:
    three axis rings

SCALE:
    three endpoint cubes

Do NOT import TransformControls.

The RIG primitive gizmo is already working and proven in this exact application.

Use the same:

    axis colours
    depthTest:false
    high renderOrder
    gizmoAxis metadata
    screen-space drag projection

Do not fork the maths unnecessarily.

==================================================
7. GIZMO POSITION FOR SINGLE SELECTION
==================================================

For one selected object:

    gizmo.position = selected object's world position

Use the live mesh group's position where appropriate so the gizmo matches what
the user sees.

Do not derive it from stale React state inside the render loop if meshMap has
the authoritative live transform.

==================================================
8. GIZMO POSITION FOR MULTI-SELECTION
==================================================

For more than one selected object:

    gizmo position = arithmetic centroid of selected objects' WORLD positions

Example:

    cx = sum(x) / N
    cy = sum(y) / N
    cz = sum(z) / N

This gives the Blender-style MEDIAN visual pivot.

Do not place the gizmo only on selId when several objects are selected.

selId remains the active/anchor object for the OBJECT inspector and existing
selection logic.

The gizmo is a GROUP-selection affordance.

==================================================
9. IMPORTANT LIMITATION: MEDIAN VISUAL PIVOT != FULL BLENDER PIVOT YET
==================================================

Do not silently change multi-object rotation/scale semantics in this build.

Current updateObj multi-selection semantics are:

MOVE:
    same positional delta applied to all selected objects

ROTATE:
    same rx/ry delta applied to each object's own rotation

SCALE:
    each object's own scale multiplied by the same ratio

That means current rotation/scale is effectively:

    individual origins with shared delta

NOT:

    rotate/scale object positions around the median point

Preserve those semantics in this pass.

The gizmo may visually sit at the centroid for consistency, but report this
limitation clearly.

True Blender median-pivot rotation/scale is a separate later feature requiring:

    relative vector from centroid
    rotate that vector
    update x/y/z
    update object rotation
    equivalent group-scale position maths

Do not smuggle that geometry change into this UI port.

==================================================
10. SINGLE CLICK SELECTION MUST UPDATE BOTH selId AND selIds
==================================================

The existing viewport pick currently does:

    setSelId(hid)

but does not maintain the full multi-selection Set in the same way the layer
list does.

Fix this while adding the gizmo.

Normal click on viewport object:

    setSelId(hid)
    setSelIds(new Set([hid]))

CTRL/CMD click:

    toggle hid in selIds
    set selId = hid when appropriate

SHIFT semantics should NOT conflict with current Shift+drag-Y behaviour.

Do not overload Shift-click range selection in 3D; there is no meaningful
linear range in viewport space.

Use:

    plain click = single select
    Ctrl/Cmd click = additive/toggle select

Keep Shift reserved for current Y manipulation unless deliberately changed.

==================================================
11. EMPTY-SPACE CLICK
==================================================

When the user clicks empty Scene space WITHOUT beginning orbit:

desired selection behaviour:

    clear selId
    clear selIds
    hide gizmo

But be careful not to make every orbit drag deselect unexpectedly.

Recommended:

    distinguish click from drag by movement threshold

For example:

    pointer/mouse down records position

if released with movement < 3-5px and no hit:
    deselect

if moved:
    orbit as usual
    keep selection

Do not destroy selection merely because the user rotates the camera.

==================================================
12. GIZMO HANDLES TAKE PRIORITY OVER MODEL PICKING
==================================================

Follow the RIG implementation.

At start of Scene onDown:

A.
    if gizmo visible:
        raycast gizmo handles FIRST

B.
    if handle hit:
        begin gizmo drag
        do NOT object-pick
        do NOT orbit
        do NOT direct-drag model

C.
    otherwise:
        fall through to current model picking logic

Use a:

    _sceneGizmoBusy

or equivalent flag so the camera drag path cannot run simultaneously.

==================================================
13. PORT THE SCREEN-SPACE AXIS PROJECTION
==================================================

Reuse the proven RIG method.

For selected axis:

    project gizmo origin to screen
    project a point +axis to screen
    subtract
    normalize

Then project mouse delta onto that screen direction.

This prevents axis motion reversing just because the camera has orbited around
the object.

Do not invent another drag mapping.

==================================================
14. ALL GIZMO MUTATION GOES THROUGH updateObj
==================================================

This is critical.

For one selected anchor id:

MOVE X:

    const obj = objsRef.current.find(...)
    updateObj(anchorId, 'x', obj.x + delta)

MOVE Y:

    updateObj(anchorId, 'y', obj.y + delta)

MOVE Z:

    updateObj(anchorId, 'z', obj.z + delta)

ROTATE:

    x -> rx
    y -> ry

Inspect whether rz is supported consistently before exposing Z rotation.

Current updateObj multi-selection whitelist is:

    x, y, z, scale, rx, ry

It does NOT currently include:

    rz

Do not expose a Z rotation handle that only rotates the anchor.

Either:

    add rz safely to updateObj's multi-transform whitelist

if scene objects/generator already support rz,

OR

    temporarily disable Z rotation and report why.

Do not create inconsistent multi-select behaviour.

SCALE:

    updateObj(anchorId, 'scale', newScale)

Because updateObj already handles selIdsRef, the same call applies the correct
delta/ratio to every selected object.

==================================================
15. FIX THE EXISTING DIRECT VIEWPORT DRAG PATH
==================================================

Current X/Z and Y viewport dragging bypasses updateObj.

That contradicts the architecture comment and means multi-selection behaviour
can diverge between:

    toolbar/gizmo
    direct drag
    OBJECT fields

Fix it in this same pass because otherwise we will have two transform engines.

For current X/Z direct drag, instead of directly:

    grp.position...
    objsRef mutation
    setScene(...)

calculate nx/nz and call:

    updateObj(dragId, 'x', ...)
    updateObj(dragId, 'z', ...)

For Shift-Y drag:

    updateObj(dragId, 'y', ...)

Be careful about repeated setState calls during movement.

If two updateObj calls per move for X/Z cause stale-state or double multi-delta
problems, add a purpose-built:

    updateObjTransform(id, {x, z})

that centralises one transaction.

But do NOT maintain a separate direct-manipulation state writer.

One canonical transform path is the goal.

==================================================
16. SNAP
==================================================

Because gizmo MOVE goes through updateObj:

    existing snapOn
    existing snapStep

should apply automatically.

Do not duplicate snap maths in the gizmo.

This is one of the main reasons to route through updateObj.

Rotation and scale remain unsnapped unless there is a separate intentional
angle/scale snap feature later.

==================================================
17. LOCKED / HIDDEN
==================================================

A selected object that becomes:

    hidden
or
    locked

should not retain an interactive gizmo.

Hide the gizmo when the active selection has no transformable objects.

If some of a multi-selection are locked, decide explicitly:

preferred:
    transform only unlocked selected objects

But do not change existing locked-selection semantics without inspecting how
updateObj treats them.

At minimum, do not allow clicking a locked mesh via the viewport; current code
already correctly excludes it.

==================================================
